# Merge strategies for generated artefacts.
#
# Both snapshot/amc_lib.c and src/amc_lib.c are produced by gen_test
# from the .am sources, so any merge conflict in them is noise — the
# canonical resolution is "rebuild after merge". `merge=ours` tells
# git to keep our version on a merge conflict; the post-merge
# `./build_amc.sh` + `./tools/save-snapshot.sh` then regenerate the
# correct content from the merged sources.
#
# Without this, every cross-PR merge (which happens often when the
# LLM-driven workflows + parser fixes land back-to-back) produced a
# 5-step manual resolution dance: `git checkout --theirs` → rebuild
# → re-snapshot → amend. Now it's a single rebuild + one amend.
#
# Note: `merge=ours` requires `git config merge.ours.driver true` once
# per clone (this `.gitattributes` declares the strategy; git needs to
# know that "ours" is a real driver). The post-clone hint lives in
# CONTINUATION.md.

snapshot/amc_lib.c    merge=ours
snapshot/INFO.md      merge=ours
src/amc_lib.c         merge=ours

# Mark generated artefacts so GitHub's diff view collapses them and
# linguist excludes them from the language stats.
snapshot/amc_lib.c    linguist-generated=true
src/amc_lib.c         linguist-generated=true
runtime/*             linguist-vendored=true

# Force LF line endings for source / build artefacts on every
# platform. Without this, Windows clones with `core.autocrlf=true`
# get CRLF in .am / .sh files, and the Amalgame lexer's line
# counter drifts (CR is whitespace, but mixed CRLF + standalone CR
# in long strings shifts the reported source positions). Surfaced
# in v0.7.5 release.yml when toml.am's parse fail-reported lines
# 788/801 on a 787-line file. Affected suffixes:
*.am          text eol=lf
*.sh          text eol=lf
*.c           text eol=lf
*.h           text eol=lf
*.md          text eol=lf
*.toml        text eol=lf
*.yml         text eol=lf
*.yaml        text eol=lf
